course: everyday
lesson: 364
title: Giggles, skipping and the collection plate
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: As the collection plate went past in church in Boston, Matthew slipped out a coin to buy sweets after the service. Nobody saw. But he did not buy the sweets: the coin felt heavy in his pocket all week, and the next Sunday he could not look at the plate. On Saturday he went to confession and told the priest. The priest said he was forgiven, gave him a short prayer as penance, and asked him to put the coin back. Matthew put it back with one of his own, and walked home feeling light, as if he had put down a rucksack full of stones.
explain: At her first confession Grace was so ashamed of one thing — she had pinched her little brother hard and made him cry — that she left it out. She felt worse afterwards, not lighter. A few days later she went back and told the priest everything. He said gently «Thank you for being brave. You are forgiven», and that time the weight really went. She said sorry to her brother too.
explain: Church is a place where people pray, listen and keep quiet together. Giggling, kicking the pew, playing on a phone or running up the aisle spoils it for others, and can even be dangerous near candles.
explain: Other Sundays went wrong in smaller ways. Lily giggled all through the prayers at her friend's funny face, so she was moved to sit next to Dad, away from her friend. Ben ate the sweets saved for the Sunday school prize, then owned up and brought a new bag from his pocket money. Sam ran up the aisle and knocked over the candles: someone could have been burnt, and the cloth was spoiled with wax; he said sorry to the vicar (the priest in some churches) and helped scrape off the wax. Noah kicked the pew all through the sermon (the priest's long talk); next time he tried to listen for one thing the vicar said.
explain: Confession brings forgiveness and real relief, but it works only when you are honest, and it does not replace putting it right with the people you wronged.
fact: The collection plate gathers money given to the church, often for people who need help and for running the church.
fact: In confession a person tells their sins honestly to a priest, who gives absolution and usually a small penance, like a prayer.
fact: Leaving out a sin on purpose in confession usually leaves the guilt in place.
fact: Many people feel real relief and lightness after an honest confession.
fact: After confession, you still give back what you took and say sorry to the person you hurt.
fact: Giggling during prayers can disturb people who are praying around you.
fact: Lit candles and running children are a fire and burn risk.
fact: Making fun of how someone sings hurts them, even if they sing loudly.
fact: Treats saved for a whole group belong to the group, not to whoever finds them.
fact: Hiding from where you are meant to be makes adults worry and adds a lie to the skipping.
exercise: Why did Matthew take a coin from the collection plate? | He wanted sweets after church | The priest offered it to him | He thought it was his own coin | He was giving it to a friend
exercise: Nobody saw Matthew. Why did he not enjoy the sweets? | The coin felt heavy on his conscience | The sweet shop was closed for the whole week | He did not like sweets | Mum found the coin in his pocket and took it away
exercise: What did the priest ask Matthew to do after confession? | Put the coin back | Keep the coin as a reminder | Tell the whole church | Never come to church again
exercise: Why did Matthew put back a coin of his own as well? | To make up for what he took, and more | Because the priest charged a fee for the confession | To buy a better seat | Because coins come in pairs
exercise: Grace left her worst sin out of confession. How did she feel afterwards? | Worse, not lighter | Light and happy, as if a weight had gone | Proud of herself | Nothing at all
exercise: What made the weight go for Grace in the end? | Going back and telling everything honestly | Forgetting about it | Telling her best friend all about it instead | Doing more chores
exercise: Even after confession, what else did Grace need to do? | Say sorry to her brother | Nothing, she was forgiven | Tell her teacher | Write a letter to the priest
exercise: Tom skipped Sunday school and hid in the park. What happened? | The teacher asked Mum, and Mum was worried and sad | Nobody noticed he was gone, so nothing happened | He got a prize for football | The teacher thanked him
exercise: What would have been better than hiding, if Tom did not want to go? | Saying so honestly to Mum | Hiding somewhere warmer | Pretending to be ill | Going for five minutes only
exercise: Lily giggled all through the prayers because her friend pulled a face. What was the fair consequence? | She sat next to Dad, away from her friend | She was sent out of church to stand in the porch | She was given a sweet to stop | Her friend was blamed instead
exercise: Jake played a game on his phone during the service. Mum saw the glow and held out her hand. What did he learn? | Some times are for screens and some for quiet | Phones are allowed if quiet | Church is boring, so games on the phone are fine | Only adults can use phones in church
exercise: Ella made fun of the old lady who sings loudly in the choir. What put it right? | Saying sorry to her after the service | Singing even louder than her to drown her out | Saying it was only her cousin | Asking her to sing quietly
exercise: Ben ate the sweets saved for the Sunday school prize. What did he do to make it right? | Owned up and brought a new bag from his pocket money | Said the bag was already open, so the sweets were free | Blamed the teacher for leaving them out | Shared the wrappers with the class
exercise: Sam ran up the aisle and knocked over the candles. Why is that serious? | Someone could have been burnt and the cloth was spoiled | Candles are cheap and easy to light | It only made a small noise, so it did not really matter | The vicar likes wax on the floor
exercise: How did Sam help put it right? | Said sorry to the vicar and helped scrape off the wax | Ran out of the church before anyone saw what he did | Lit the candles again | Blamed the wind
exercise: Noah kicked the pew all through a long sermon. What would have been a better way to cope with boredom? | Try to listen for one thing the vicar says | Kick more softly | Play a quiet game on his phone under the pew | Leave without telling anyone
test: mate during prayers pulls a face, «don't laugh, don't laugh…» you snort. Best move? | Look away, breathe, say sorry after | Laugh louder, it's funny anyway | Pull a face back | Leave the church
test: «took 50p off the plate last week and no one saw, it worked last time, so it's fine». Is it? | No, it was taken from the church; give it back | Yes, if no one saw you take it then it's fine | Yes, churches have loads | Only if you put it in next week
test: Priest: «Is there anything else you want to tell me?» You've left out the worst one. What's right? | Tell him that too | Say no and hurry out | Tell him next year | Tell him that someone else did the worst one
test: «I went to confession so I don't have to say sorry to my brother now». What's true? | You still need to say sorry to him | Confession covers everyone, including your brother | Brothers don't need sorrys | Only if he asks
test: «Sunday school's boring, I'm hiding in the park, cover for me». Best answer? | No; tell Mum honestly you don't want to go | OK, I'll tell your mum that you're ill | OK, but only this week | Hide with him
test: Mum whispers «Phone. Now.» in the middle of the service. What's the best response? | Hand it over and say sorry later | Put it in your pocket and keep playing | Say you were reading the Bible | Turn the brightness down
test: cousin copying the loud choir lady and everyone's sniggering. She turns round. Best move? | Stop, and say sorry to her after | Copy her too | Pretend you're coughing so she doesn't notice | Blame your cousin loudly
test: sweets for the prize are on the table, bag's open. «no one'll notice a few». What's right? | Leave them; they're for the prize | Take a few, it's an open bag anyway | Take one each for your friends | Swap them for worse sweets
test: after church kids racing up the aisle near the lit candles. What's the risk? | Burns or fire if a candle falls | No risk, candles are stuck down | The carpet gets dusty | The vicar gets out of breath
test: After an honest confession, Matthew feels light. His sister asks «so you can keep the coin now?». Best answer? | No, I still give it back | Yes, I'm forgiven | Yes, God doesn't mind coins | Only if it's small
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a sin deliberately omitted from confession tends to leave the penitent troubled. | The guilt is not addressed, and the relief of honest confession is not attained | The priest is unable to hear any confession which has been only partial | Omitted sins are automatically forgiven | Confession has no effect upon guilt
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between absolution and the restitution of a coin taken from a collection? | Absolution does not remove the obligation to restore what was taken | Absolution transfers ownership of the coin to the penitent | Restitution is required only in the case where the taking is discovered | Absolution and restitution are mutually exclusive
exam: It is given that a child, unwilling to attend Sunday school, concealed himself in a park. Select the response consistent with honesty. | The child ought to have stated his unwillingness openly to his parent | The child acted prudently in concealing himself | The child ought to have feigned an illness in order to avoid the class | The child bears no responsibility for the parent's worry
exam: State the principal hazard arising from children running in proximity to lit candles. | The risk of burns and of fire | The risk of extinguishing the candles | The risk of disturbing the dust | No appreciable hazard
